\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Why Figure 3 of Wang & Haiting Cannot Be Reproduced to a Small Error
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Bampaliaris Nikolaos
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
12-10-2026
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Summary
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figure 3 of the Wang & Haiting paper (the axial fields 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{I}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}+E_{I}$
\end_inset
 near the rod tip) was reproduced with our implementation of the paper's Eqs. (4) and (5), and compared point by point with the curves digitized from the published figure. The model reproduces the 
\series bold
qualitative
\series default
 behaviour of all three curves, but a 
\series bold
quantitative
\series default
 gap remains, mainly in the rod image field 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{I}$
\end_inset
 close to the tip. This note shows that the gap is not caused by the digitization of the figure, cannot be explained by a different applied (Laplacian) field, and cannot be removed by any physically consistent choice of the unreported parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset
. It must therefore originate from information that the paper does not report.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
What the Paper Reports and What It Does Not
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For Figure 3 the paper states: a rod-plane air gap of length 
\begin_inset Formula $D=80\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
, rod tip radius 
\begin_inset Formula $R=0.1\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
, first-corona inception voltage 
\begin_inset Formula $V=27.5\,\text{kV}$
\end_inset
, and streamer length 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{cs}=2.7\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
. For the charge density 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho(r)=A/(r+C)^{B}$
\end_inset
 it states only that “A is chosen to satisfy (a)” (the field in the streamer region is about 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{c}=5\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
), that “B and C are chosen to meet (b)” (the field in the streamer region is nearly uniform), and that “usually, B is 1.5 and C is about 0.035”.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following quantities, all of which change the curves of Figure 3, are 
\series bold
not
\series default
 reported:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the cone boundary angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
 of the space-charge region;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
, or the exact procedure used to obtain it from condition (a);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the exact values of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset
 used for Figure 3 (only typical values are given);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the charge layout of the Charge Simulation Method (CSM) used for the applied field 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{ex}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Our Reproduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the procedure for 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 is not given, we close the model as follows. The total streamer charge is obtained from Gauss's law over the cross-section of the cone head,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
Q_{cs}=\varepsilon_{0}E_{c}\,\pi\left[L_{cs}\tan\left(\theta_{B}/2\right)\right]^{2},\label{eq:qcs}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 follows from the volume integral of the density over the cone,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
A=\frac{Q_{cs}}{2\pi\left[1-\cos\left(\theta_{B}/2\right)\right]\int_{0}^{L_{cs}}\frac{r^{2}}{(r+C)^{B}}\,dr}.\label{eq:A}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
 is then solved self-consistently so that the total field at the streamer tip equals the streamer field:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
E_{ex}(L_{cs})+E_{sp}(L_{cs})+E_{I,rod}(L_{cs})+E_{I,plane}(L_{cs})=E_{c},\label{eq:theta_condition}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{ex}$
\end_inset
 computed by our own CSM implementation. With 
\begin_inset Formula $B=1.5$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $C=0.035\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{c}=5\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 this gives 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}=93.39^{\circ}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{cs}=11.41\,\text{nC}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{ex}(L_{cs})=1.00\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
. Our integrands were checked against the paper's Eqs. (4) and (5): 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset
 is measured from the tip apex, 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{2}=\sqrt{(R+r\cos\theta)^{2}+(r\sin\theta)^{2}}$
\end_inset
, the Kelvin image lies at 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{3}=R^{2}/r_{2}$
\end_inset
 with charge factor 
\begin_inset Formula $R/r_{2}$
\end_inset
, and the axial projection uses 
\begin_inset Formula $\cos\theta_{4}$
\end_inset
. The implementation therefore follows the published equations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Digitizing Figure 3 and Its Error
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The three curves were extracted with WebPlotDigitizer (69 to 88 points per curve). The axes were calibrated with the origin 
\begin_inset Formula $(0,0)$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $z=3\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 on the horizontal axis, and 
\begin_inset Formula $E=5$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E=-50\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 on the vertical axis. Two error sources were quantified:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Random click error.
\series default
 The deviation of each digitized point from the line through its two neighbours gives a scatter of only 
\begin_inset Formula $\pm0.14$
\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\pm0.32\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Calibration error.
\series default
 The vertical axis of the hand-drawn figure is not uniformly spaced. Measured from a 400 dpi scan, the spacing is 
\begin_inset Formula $11.6\,\text{px}$
\end_inset
 per kV/cm between 
\begin_inset Formula $5$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $12.4\,\text{px}$
\end_inset
 per kV/cm between 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $-20$
\end_inset
, but only 
\begin_inset Formula $9.8\,\text{px}$
\end_inset
 per kV/cm between 
\begin_inset Formula $-40$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $-50$
\end_inset
. A two-point calibration assumes uniform spacing, so values in the middle of the axis are read up to about 
\begin_inset Formula $1.6\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 too negative (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:calibration"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
). The digitized data were corrected using the measured tick positions.
\end_layout

