\begin_layout Standard
Three observations rule out the applied field as the cause:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
No single angle fits all curves.
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 fits best near 
\begin_inset Formula $110^{\circ}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{I}$
\end_inset
 near 
\begin_inset Formula $130^{\circ}$
\end_inset
, and the error of 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}+E_{I}$
\end_inset
 never falls below about 
\begin_inset Formula $12\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Better-fitting angles are unphysical.
\series default
 Above about 
\begin_inset Formula $101^{\circ}$
\end_inset
 the space charge alone exceeds 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{c}$
\end_inset
 at the streamer tip, so 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{ex}(L_{cs})$
\end_inset
 would have to be negative, i.e. pointing towards the rod, which is impossible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Realistic variations of the applied field are small.
\series default
 The analytical hyperboloid approximation 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z)\approx2V/\left[(R+2z)\ln(4D/R)\right]$
\end_inset
 gives 
\begin_inset Formula $1.24\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $z=L_{cs}$
\end_inset
, close to our CSM value of 
\begin_inset Formula $1.00\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
. A difference of this size shifts 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
 by only a few degrees and leaves the error practically unchanged.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Free Fit of the Unreported Parameters
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As a final test, 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset
 were all left free (
\begin_inset Formula $B=1.5$
\end_inset
) and fitted to the corrected 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{I}$
\end_inset
 curves by least squares. Since every field is proportional to 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
, the optimal 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 for given 
\begin_inset Formula $(\theta_{B},C)$
\end_inset
 has the closed form 
\begin_inset Formula $A=\mathbf{g}\cdot\mathbf{y}/\mathbf{g}\cdot\mathbf{g}$
\end_inset
, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{g}$
\end_inset
 are the model fields for 
\begin_inset Formula $A=1$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{y}$
\end_inset
 the digitized values. A coarse grid over 
\begin_inset Formula $(\theta_{B},C)$
\end_inset
 followed by a Nelder-Mead refinement gives the best fit in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:fit"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
.
\end_layout

